Linux Host Reboot API Workflow
1. Purpose
This document describes the end-to-end workflow for rebooting a Linux host through the Self-Service Portal using the Linux Host Reboot API. It combines the portal process and backend API execution into a single workflow.
2. Scope
This workflow enables authorized users to reboot Linux servers through a self-service interface without manual intervention from the operations team.
3. Components
Self-Service Portal
API Gateway
Authentication & Authorization Service
Input Validation Service
Request Group Creation Service
Linux Host Reboot API
Target Linux Server
Status Monitoring Service
4. Workflow
Step 1: Access Self-Service Portal
The user logs into the Self-Service Portal using valid credentials.
Step 2: Select Linux Host Reboot
Navigate to the Linux Host Reboot service.
Step 3: Enter Server Details
Provide the required server information such as:
Hostname
Environment
Business Justification (if required)
Step 4: Submit Request
Click Submit to initiate the reboot request.
Step 5: API Receives Request
The API Gateway receives the request from the portal.
Step 6: Authentication & Authorization
The system validates:
User identity
User permissions
Access to the requested server
If validation fails, the request is rejected.
Step 7: Input Validation
The system verifies:
Hostname exists
Server is eligible for reboot
Mandatory fields are completed
Invalid requests are returned with an error.
Step 8: Create Request Group
A request group is created to track the execution.
Step 9: Execute Linux Reboot
The Linux Host Reboot API sends the reboot command to the target Linux server.
Step 10: Monitor Execution
The execution engine monitors the reboot progress.
Step 11: Update Status
The portal updates the request status based on the execution result.
Possible statuses:
SUBMITTED
IN PROGRESS
SUCCESS
UNKNOWN
PROVIDER FAILED
Step 12: User Verification
The user monitors the request until completion.
